.TH SET_HAS 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_has \- Check if an element exists in a set

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_has(const set_t* set, const void* val, const size_t size);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_has\fR function checks if an element exists in a set. It takes three arguments: the set, a pointer to the element, and the size of the element. The function returns 1 if the element exists in the set, or 0 otherwise.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set in which to check for the element.
.TP
.IB val
A pointer to the element to check.
.TP
.IB size
The size of the element.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the element exists in the set, or 0 otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_has\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    char* element = "apple";

    set_init(&set);
    set_add(&set, element, strlen(element));

    if (set_has(&set, element, strlen(element))) {
        printf("Element exists in set\n");
    } else {
        printf("Element does not exist in set\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef